"""Software engineering and computer science intents."""

ROWS = [
    # ---- fundamentals ----
    ("an algorithm", "An algorithm is a finite, precise recipe for solving a problem — a sequence of steps that takes an input and produces an output. Sorting a list, finding a route, or compressing a photo are all algorithms, and their efficiency is measured in time and memory. 🧮", {"keys": ["algorithms"]}),
    ("a data structure", "A data structure is a way of organizing data so the operations you need are fast — arrays for indexing, hash tables for lookup, trees for ordered data, queues for fairness. Choosing the right one usually matters more than clever code. 🗂️", {"keys": ["data structures"]}),
    ("an array", "An array is a block of values stored in order, accessed by index in constant time: arr[0] is instant. Its strength (random access) comes with a weakness: inserting or deleting in the middle means shifting everything after it. 📚", {"keys": ["arrays"]}),
    ("a linked list", "A linked list stores each value in a node that points to the next. Insertion and deletion are cheap — just rewire the pointers — but reaching the 500th item means walking 500 links, which is why arrays usually win in practice despite worse insertion theory. 🔗", {"keys": ["linked lists"]}),
    ("a stack data structure", "A stack is last-in, first-out: push adds to the top, pop removes from the top. It mirrors undo history, browser back buttons, and function calls — the call stack itself is a stack. 🥞", {"keys": ["stack data structure", "pushdown stack"]}),
    ("a queue", "A queue is first-in, first-out: enqueue at the back, dequeue at the front. Printers, task schedulers, and message systems use queues because fairness means first come, first served. 🎫", {"keys": ["queues"]}),
    ("a tree data structure", "A tree is a hierarchy of nodes with parent–child links and no cycles — file systems, HTML pages, and organization charts are trees. A binary tree limits each node to two children, which makes searching and sorting fast when it stays balanced. 🌳", {"keys": ["tree data structure", "binary tree", "binary search tree"]}),
    ("a heap", "A heap is a tree-shaped structure that keeps the smallest (or largest) element at the top, so finding it takes O(1) and inserting takes O(log n). Heaps power priority queues: what a scheduler runs next, what a pathfinder explores first. ⛰️", {"keys": ["heaps", "priority queue"]}),
    ("a hash table", "A hash table maps keys to values using a hash function, giving average O(1) lookup, insert, and delete. It is the workhorse behind dictionaries, caches, database indexes, and counting things in one pass — the single most useful data structure in practice. 🗝️", {"keys": ["hash tables", "hash map", "dictionary data structure"]}),
    ("a graph data structure", "A graph is a set of nodes connected by edges — social networks, road maps, dependency trees, the internet. Classic problems: shortest path (Dijkstra), connectivity, and topological ordering of tasks. 🕸️", {"keys": ["graphs", "graph theory"]}),
    ("binary search", "Binary search finds an item in a sorted list by repeatedly halving the search space: a million items takes about 20 comparisons. The catch — the list must be sorted, and off-by-one errors are the classic bug. 🔍", {"keys": ["binary search algorithm"]}),
    ("sorting algorithms", "Sorting puts items in order, and the classic options trade speed for simplicity: quicksort averages O(n log n) and is fast in practice; merge sort guarantees O(n log n) and is stable; insertion sort is fine for tiny arrays. No comparison sort beats O(n log n) in the worst case — that is proven. 🔢", {"keys": ["sorting", "sort algorithm"]}),
    ("big o notation", "Big O notation describes how an algorithm's cost grows with input size: O(1) constant, O(log n) halving, O(n) linear, O(n²) quadratic. It ignores constants and hardware, focusing on the shape of the curve — which is why an O(n log n) sort beats O(n²) once n gets big. 📈", {"keys": ["big o", "big-o", "complexity"]}),
    ("recursion", "Recursion is a function that calls itself on a smaller version of the problem until it hits a base case — like Russian dolls. It is elegant for trees and divide-and-conquer algorithms, but each pending call consumes stack memory, so deep recursion can overflow. 🔁", {"keys": ["recursive"]}),
    ("dynamic programming", "Dynamic programming solves problems that overlap into subproblems by storing each answer once and reusing it — memoization. It turns exponential brute force into polynomial time for problems like edit distance, knapsack, and shortest paths. 🧠", {"keys": ["memoization", "dp"]}),
    ("a greedy algorithm", "A greedy algorithm builds a solution by always taking the best-looking step right now. Sometimes that is provably optimal (like minimum coins for certain denominations), sometimes it quietly fails — which is why greedy solutions need proof, not vibes. 🪙", {"keys": ["greedy algorithms"]}),
    ("divide and conquer", "Divide and conquer splits a problem into smaller independent pieces, solves each recursively, and combines the results — merge sort and binary search are the classics. The hard part is usually the combining step. ➗", {"keys": ["divide-and-conquer"]}),
    ("a compiler", "A compiler translates source code into machine code ahead of time, optimizing as it goes: it can spend milliseconds to save hours of runtime. C, Rust, and Go compile; Python and JavaScript traditionally interpret — though modern engines do both, JIT-compiling hot paths. ⚙️", {"keys": ["compilers", "compilation"]}),
    ("an interpreter", "An interpreter executes source code directly, line by line, without a separate build step — which makes development fast and incremental. Python, Ruby, and JavaScript engines are interpreters (with JIT tricks); the price is usually slower raw execution than a compiler. 🔄", {"keys": ["interpreters", "interpreted language"]}),
    ("bytecode", "Bytecode is an intermediate instruction set between source code and machine code — compact, portable, and executed by a virtual machine. Java compiles to JVM bytecode, Python to `.pyc` files, JavaScript engines to their own internal bytecode. 🧱", {"keys": ["byte code", "virtual machine"]}),

    # ---- systems ----
    ("an operating system", "An operating system is the software that manages a computer's hardware and gives programs a safe place to run: it schedules CPU time, allocates memory, talks to disks and networks, and enforces permissions. Windows, macOS, Linux, Android, and iOS are all operating systems. 🖥️", {"keys": ["os", "operating systems"]}),
    ("the kernel", "The kernel is the core of an operating system — the privileged code that talks directly to hardware and enforces the rules between processes. A kernel panic or blue screen means it hit a state it cannot safely handle. ⚙️"),
    ("memory", "Computer memory is the hierarchy of storage: registers (nanoseconds), cache (tens of nanoseconds), RAM (about 100 nanoseconds), SSD (microseconds), disk and network (milliseconds). Programs are fast when their working data stays near the top of that list. 🧠", {"keys": ["ram", "computer memory"]}),
    ("a cpu", "The CPU is a computer's main processor: it fetches instructions, decodes them, and executes them billions of times per second, with multiple cores handling different workstreams. Modern CPUs also predict branches and cache aggressively to keep their pipelines full. 🖲️", {"keys": ["processor", "central processing unit"]}),
    ("a gpu", "A GPU is a processor built for parallelism: thousands of small cores that run many operations at once. It exists for graphics, but it turned out that neural networks are giant piles of parallel math, which is why the AI boom runs on GPUs (and increasingly TPUs and NPUs). 🎮", {"keys": ["graphics card", "video card"]}),
    ("a transistor", "A transistor is a tiny electronic switch — a few nanometers wide in modern chips and billions of them per processor. Turning it on or off represents a 1 or 0; everything a computer does is an avalanche of these switches flipping in patterns. 🔌", {"keys": ["transistors"]}),
    ("a process", "A process is a running program with its own isolated memory space and resources. Isolation is the point: one crashed process should not take down the others, which is why browsers put each tab in its own process. 🏃", {"keys": ["processes"]}),
    ("a thread", "A thread is the smallest unit of execution inside a process — lightweight, sharing memory with its siblings. Multiple threads let a program do several things at once, but shared memory means the danger is real: unsynchronized threads produce bugs that vanish when you look at them. 🧵", {"keys": ["threads", "multithreading"]}),
    ("concurrency", "Concurrency is structuring a program to handle multiple tasks in overlapping time — not necessarily simultaneously. It is how a server handles thousands of connections and a UI stays responsive; the hard parts are race conditions, deadlocks, and making timeouts behave. 🔀", {"keys": ["concurrent"]}),
    ("parallelism", "Parallelism is actually doing multiple computations at the same instant on different cores or machines. Concurrency is about structure; parallelism is about speed — and Amdahl's law says the serial parts of a task cap how much speedup the parallel parts can buy. ⚡", {"keys": ["parallel computing"]}),
    ("the terminal", "The terminal (or command line) is a text interface to your computer: instead of clicking icons, you type commands. It feels archaic until you need to rename a thousand files or automate a deployment — then it is the fastest tool you own. ⌨️", {"keys": ["command line", "cli", "console"]}),
    ("a shell", "A shell is the program inside a terminal that interprets your commands and runs scripts — bash, zsh, PowerShell, and fish are shells. It is a whole programming language with the strange quirk that it is optimized for launching other programs. 🐚", {"keys": ["shells", "bash", "powershell"]}),
    ("a script", "A script is a small program written to automate a task — rename files, deploy a site, scrape a report. Scripts are usually written in interpreted languages (Python, JavaScript, shell) because the priority is fast iteration, not peak performance. 📜", {"keys": ["scripting", "scripts"]}),
    ("git", "Git is a distributed version control system: it records snapshots of a project, lets you branch off experiments, merge work back, and reconstruct history. Created by Linus Torvalds in 2005 for the Linux kernel, it now runs in almost every software project on Earth. 🗂️", {"keys": ["git version control"]}),
    ("version control", "Version control is the practice of tracking how files change over time — who changed what, when, and why — so you can undo mistakes, compare versions, and collaborate without emailing zip files around. Git is the dominant tool; the concept is the important part. 📚", {"keys": ["source control"]}),
    ("merging", "A merge combines changes from two branches. When both branches touched the same lines, Git asks you to resolve a conflict — a manual choice about which version wins (or how to blend them). Merges preserve history; that is their feature and their cost. 🔀", {"keys": ["merge conflict", "merge conflicts"]}),
    ("rebasing", "Rebasing moves your commits onto a new base, rewriting them as if you had started there — a clean, linear history. The golden rule: never rebase commits that other people already have, because rewriting published history breaks their copy. 📏", {"keys": ["rebase"]}),
    ("a pull request", "A pull request (PR) is a proposal to merge a branch into the main codebase, with the diff, discussion, and automated checks attached. It is where code review happens in modern teams — the social layer of version control. 🔍", {"keys": ["pull requests", "pr", "merge request"]}),
    ("github", "GitHub is a hosting platform for Git repositories with collaboration tools on top: pull requests, issues, code review, actions. It made Git social, and it is where most of the open-source world keeps its work — including Farell's projects. 🐙", {"keys": ["github platform"]}),
    ("open source", "Open source software publishes its source code under licenses that let anyone study, modify, and redistribute it. The movement produced Linux, Python, and the web itself — and its economics work because the code is not the scarce part; support, hosting, and expertise are. 🌍", {"keys": ["oss"]}),
    ("a software license", "A software license is the legal terms that say what others may do with your code. Permissive licenses like MIT and Apache allow almost anything; copyleft licenses like the GPL require derivatives to stay open. Without a license, others technically have no right to use it at all. ⚖️", {"keys": ["open source license", "mit license", "gpl"]}),
    ("semantic versioning", "Semantic versioning numbers releases like MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH: increment MAJOR for breaking changes, MINOR for backward-compatible features, PATCH for fixes. It lets tools and humans reason about risk before upgrading — when everyone follows it, which is the eternal caveat. 🏷️", {"keys": ["semver", "version numbers"]}),
    ("an ide", "An IDE (integrated development environment) bundles a code editor with debugging, building, refactoring, and version-control tools. VS Code, JetBrains IDEs, and Xcode are common ones; the debate between heavyweight IDE and lean editor never ends, because it is really about your habits. 🧰", {"keys": ["integrated development environment", "code editor"]}),

    # ---- quality & process ----
    ("debugging", "Debugging is finding why code misbehaves: reproduce the bug, form a hypothesis about the cause, test it, and fix the actual mechanism — not the symptom. The best debugging tool is a failing test that isolates the problem; the worst is changing things until the error disappears. 🐛", {"keys": ["debug", "bugs"]}),
    ("logging", "Logging records what a program did so you can reconstruct events later — especially the events you did not anticipate. Good logs answer \"what happened at 03:17 when it broke\" without a debugger attached; bad logs are a wall of noise nobody reads. 📝", {"keys": ["logs", "log files"]}),
    ("refactoring", "Refactoring improves code structure without changing behavior — renaming, extracting functions, removing duplication. It is paid for by future work: you spend minutes now so every future change costs less. Skipping it is how codebases become quicksand. 🔧", {"keys": ["refactor"]}),
    ("technical debt", "Technical debt is the accumulated cost of shortcuts: quick hacks, missing tests, copy-pasted logic. Like financial debt it can be a rational loan (ship now, pay later) — the problem is interest, when old shortcuts make every new change slower and scarier. 📉", {"keys": ["tech debt"]}),
    ("unit testing", "A unit test checks one small piece of code in isolation — a function, a class — with assertions about expected behavior. Fast and precise, they catch regressions the moment they appear, which is why professional codebases run thousands of them on every change. ✅", {"keys": ["unit tests", "unit test"]}),
    ("integration testing", "Integration tests check that separate pieces work together — a database plus an API, a payment flow end to end within the app. They are slower and messier than unit tests but catch the bugs that live exactly at the seams. 🔗", {"keys": ["integration tests"]}),
    ("end-to-end testing", "End-to-end (E2E) tests drive the real product the way a user would — clicking buttons in a real browser, submitting real forms. They are the closest thing to \"does it actually work\" and the most expensive to keep stable, so teams use them for critical journeys, not everything. 🎭", {"keys": ["e2e testing", "e2e tests"]}),
    ("test driven development", "TDD is a loop: write a failing test, make it pass with the simplest code, then refactor. It flips testing from a chore after the fact into the design tool for the code itself — and it guarantees tests exist, because they came first. 🔁", {"keys": ["tdd"]}),
    ("code coverage", "Code coverage measures what fraction of your code runs during tests. It is a useful gap-finder but a terrible goal: 100% coverage with weak assertions proves lines executed, not that behavior is correct. Pair coverage with meaningful assertions or it is theater. 📊", {"keys": ["test coverage"]}),
    ("ci cd", "CI/CD is continuous integration and continuous delivery: every change automatically builds, tests, and can deploy through a pipeline. CI catches integration problems within minutes of a commit; CD makes releases boring and reversible instead of dramatic. 🔄", {"keys": ["ci", "cd", "continuous integration", "continuous deployment"]}),
    ("agile", "Agile is a family of software practices built around short iterations, working software over documentation, and responding to change. The original 2001 manifesto is 68 words; the industry's interpretation of it has been... creative. 🔁", {"keys": ["agile development"]}),
    ("scrum", "Scrum is a common agile framework: work is done in short sprints (often two weeks) by a small team, with daily standups, a backlog, and review/retro ceremonies. It works when the ceremonies serve the work — and becomes theater when they replace thinking. 🏉", {"keys": ["scrum method", "sprint"]}),
    ("kanban", "Kanban visualizes work as cards moving across columns — to do, doing, done — with limits on how much can be in progress at once. Its core insight: the bottleneck is usually too much work in progress, not too little capacity. 📋", {"keys": ["kanban board"]}),
    ("a design pattern", "A design pattern is a reusable solution shape for a recurring problem — observer for event notifications, factory for flexible object creation, adapter for incompatible interfaces. The patterns catalogue became famous through the 1994 \"Gang of Four\" book; use them as vocabulary, not as stamps. 🧩", {"keys": ["design patterns", "gang of four"]}),
    ("object oriented programming", "Object-oriented programming organizes code into objects that bundle data with the methods that operate on it, using classes, inheritance, and polymorphism. It dominated for decades because it maps well to modeling the world — and its critics note inheritance trees can become their own maze. 🏛️", {"keys": ["oop", "classes", "inheritance"]}),
    ("functional programming", "Functional programming treats computation as evaluating functions, avoiding shared mutable state: data flows through pure functions that always return the same output for the same input. It makes concurrency safer and testing trivial — and it is quietly colonizing mainstream languages. λ", {"keys": ["fp", "pure functions"]}),
    ("a code review", "Code review is a second pair of eyes on a change before it merges: catching bugs, sharing knowledge, and keeping the codebase consistent. Good reviews discuss the code, not the coder, and distinguish \"this is broken\" from \"I would have done it differently.\" 👀", {"keys": ["code reviews", "reviewing code"]}),
    ("pair programming", "Pair programming has two developers at one workstation: one writes, one thinks ahead and reviews in real time, and they swap. It costs roughly two salaries for one stream of code — and buys fewer defects, faster onboarding, and shared understanding. 🤝", {"keys": ["pairing"]}),

    # ---- web & networking ----
    ("an api", "An API (application programming interface) is a defined contract for one program to ask another for things: endpoints, inputs, outputs, errors. It lets systems evolve independently — the browser and a server agree on the shape of requests, not on each other's internals. 🔌", {"keys": ["apis", "api endpoint"]}),
    ("rest", "REST is a style for web APIs built on HTTP verbs and URLs: GET reads, POST creates, PUT/PATCH updates, DELETE removes, each acting on resources like /users/7. It is simple, cacheable, and everywhere — which is why most public APIs claim to be RESTful, roughly. 🌐", {"keys": ["rest api", "restful"]}),
    ("graphql", "GraphQL lets clients ask for exactly the data they want in a single query, instead of calling fixed REST endpoints repeatedly. Created at Facebook in 2012, it solves over-fetching and under-fetching — at the cost of more complex servers and caching. 🔷", {"keys": ["graph ql"]}),
    ("http", "HTTP is the protocol of the web: a client sends a request (method, URL, headers, optional body), a server sends back a response with a status code and data. It is stateless — each request stands alone — which is why cookies and tokens exist. 🌐", {"keys": ["hypertext transfer protocol"]}),
    ("https", "HTTPS is HTTP inside TLS encryption: the same conversation, but scrambled so intermediaries cannot read or alter it, and certificates prove the server is who it claims to be. The padlock in your browser is this doing its job. 🔒", {"keys": ["tls", "ssl", "encryption web"]}),
    ("a web socket", "A WebSocket is a persistent, two-way connection between browser and server — both sides can push data any time, unlike HTTP's request-then-response. Chat apps, live games, and trading dashboards use them; the handshake even starts life as an HTTP request. 🔌", {"keys": ["websockets", "websocket"]}),
    ("json", "JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is a text format for structured data: objects, arrays, strings, numbers, booleans. It won the API format wars because it is readable by humans and parseable by everything — despite being less strict than XML and slower than binary formats. {} 📦", {"keys": ["json format"]}),
    ("xml", "XML is a markup format for structured data with tags and attributes. It is verbose but strictly defined and extensible, which is why it survives in configuration files, office documents, and older enterprise APIs — places where strictness beats brevity. 📄", {"keys": ["xml format"]}),
    ("yaml", "YAML is a human-friendly data format built on indentation instead of brackets — popular for configuration files like CI pipelines and Kubernetes manifests. The friendliness has a dark side: whitespace errors produce infamously confusing failures. 📄", {"keys": ["yml", "yaml format"]}),
    ("a status code", "HTTP status codes summarize a response: 2xx success (200 OK, 201 Created), 3xx redirects (301 moved, 304 not modified), 4xx client errors (400 bad request, 401 unauthorized, 404 not found), 5xx server errors (500 internal, 503 unavailable). The 404 is the one everybody meets. 🚦", {"keys": ["status codes", "404", "http status code"]}),
    ("a cookie", "A cookie is a small piece of data a website stores in your browser and receives back on every request — used for login sessions, preferences, and (historically) tracking. Limits: they are sent automatically, scoped per site, and legally regulated in many places. 🍪", {"keys": ["cookies"]}),
    ("a session", "A session is the server's memory of your ongoing visit — you log in once, and the server recognizes your subsequent requests via a cookie or token. Sessions expire for safety; \"remember me\" just means a longer-lived credential. 🔑", {"keys": ["sessions", "login session"]}),
    ("authentication and authorization", "Authentication answers \"who are you?\" (passwords, passkeys, tokens); authorization answers \"what are you allowed to do?\" (roles, permissions, scopes). Conflating them is a classic security bug — plenty of systems check identity and then forget to check rights. 🔐", {"keys": ["auth", "authentication", "authorization"]}),
    ("oauth", "OAuth is the standard for letting an app access your data on another service without giving it your password: you log in at the provider and get a scoped token instead. The \"log in with Google/GitHub\" buttons are OAuth (with a thin identity layer called OpenID Connect on top). 🔑", {"keys": ["oauth2", "openid"]}),
    ("a jwt", "A JWT (JSON Web Token) is a signed, compact token carrying claims — user id, roles, expiry — that a server can verify without a database lookup. Signed, not encrypted: anyone can read the payload, so never put secrets in one. 🎫", {"keys": ["json web token", "jwts"]}),
    ("hashing", "Hashing runs data through a one-way function to a fixed-size fingerprint — change one byte and the hash changes completely, but you cannot reverse it back. Passwords are stored as salted hashes; file checksums catch corruption and tampering. #️⃣", {"keys": ["hash function", "hashes"]}),
    ("encryption", "Encryption scrambles data so only holders of the key can read it. Symmetric encryption uses one shared key (fast, used for bulk data); asymmetric uses a public/private pair (slower, used to exchange keys and sign). HTTPS uses both together. 🔐", {"keys": ["encrypted", "cryptography"]}),
    ("two factor authentication", "2FA adds a second proof to your password: a code from an app, a hardware key, or a passkey. It blocks the vast majority of account-takeover attacks, because a stolen password alone is no longer enough. 🔐", {"keys": ["2fa", "two-factor", "mfa"]}),
    ("a password manager", "A password manager generates and stores a unique random password per site behind one master password, so a breach of one service cannot cascade into others. It is the single highest-value security habit available to normal humans. 🔑", {"keys": ["password managers"]}),
    ("dns", "DNS (the Domain Name System) is the internet's phone book: it translates human names like github.com into IP addresses. Caching at every level makes it fast; misconfiguration or poisoning makes it break — which is why \"it is always DNS\" is an industry proverb. 📖", {"keys": ["domain name system"]}),
    ("an ip address", "An IP address is a numeric label for a device on a network — IPv4 looks like 192.168.1.10, the newer IPv6 like 2001:db8::1. Addresses identify where to deliver packets, not who you are; \"your IP\" is mostly your home router or mobile carrier. 📮", {"keys": ["ip addresses", "ipv4", "ipv6"]}),
    ("tcp ip", "TCP/IP is the internet's core protocol suite: IP delivers packets between addresses (best effort), TCP orders and verifies them into a reliable stream, and applications (HTTP, email) build on top. The design philosophy: be robust, assume loss, let the edges handle complexity. 🌐", {"keys": ["tcp", "ip protocol"]}),
    ("udp", "UDP is the faster, dumber sibling of TCP: it fires packets at an address with no handshake, no ordering, no retransmission. Perfect for live video, voice calls, and games — a late frame is worthless, so mild loss beats added delay. 📡", {"keys": ["udp protocol"]}),
    ("a port", "A port is a numbered door on a machine that routes network traffic to the right program: 443 for HTTPS, 80 for HTTP, 5432 for PostgreSQL. Your computer can run dozens of listening services because each one answers on its own port. 🚪", {"keys": ["ports", "network port"]}),
    ("a firewall", "A firewall filters network traffic by rules — allow or block by address, port, or protocol. It can live in your OS (filtering a single machine) or as a dedicated appliance protecting a whole network. It is a bouncer, not a bulletproof vest. 🧱", {"keys": ["firewalls"]}),
    ("a vpn", "A VPN makes an encrypted tunnel from your device to a server, so your traffic exits from there and local networks cannot read it. Useful on hostile Wi-Fi and for remote access; the marketing claim of \"total anonymity\" is overstated — the VPN provider can see plenty, and you still log into the same accounts. 🕶️", {"keys": ["virtual private network"]}),
    ("a server", "A server is a computer (or program) that provides something to other computers: web pages, files, API responses, authentication. The word describes a role, not a shape — a laptop and a rack in a data center can both be servers. 🖧", {"keys": ["servers"]}),
    ("a client", "A client is the program that requests services — your browser is a client of web servers, your phone's mail app is a client of mail servers. Client–server is the fundamental split of networked computing. 📱", {"keys": ["clients", "client server"]}),
    ("a load balancer", "A load balancer spreads incoming traffic across several servers so no single one is overwhelmed, and routes around the ones that fail health checks. Round-robin, least-connections, and latency-aware strategies are common. 🧲", {"keys": ["load balancing"]}),
    ("a reverse proxy", "A reverse proxy sits in front of servers and forwards client requests to them, adding TLS termination, caching, compression, and routing. Nginx and Caddy are the usual suspects; your favorite website almost certainly has one in front. 🔄", {"keys": ["reverse proxies", "proxy server"]}),
    ("a cdn", "A CDN (content delivery network) caches your files at edge servers around the world, so users download from a location physically near them. It cuts latency, absorbs traffic spikes, and shields origin servers — the simplest large performance win available. 🌍", {"keys": ["content delivery network", "cdn edge"]}),
    ("caching", "Caching stores the result of an expensive operation so the next request is cheap — in browser memory, at the edge, in Redis, or in a database's query cache. The two hard problems in computer science are naming things, cache invalidation, and off-by-one errors. ⚡", {"keys": ["cache", "cached"]}),
    ("redis", "Redis is an in-memory data store used as a cache, message broker, and fast queue — everything lives in RAM, so reads and writes take microseconds. It survives restarts with optional persistence, which is enough for most caching use cases. ⚡", {"keys": ["redis cache"]}),

    # ---- data ----
    ("a database", "A database is organized storage your programs can query reliably — with transactions, concurrency control, and indexes. Two great families: relational (SQL — tables with strict schema) and non-relational (NoSQL — documents, key-value, graphs). The right choice depends on your access patterns, not fashion. 🗄️", {"keys": ["databases", "db"]}),
    ("sql", "SQL (Structured Query Language) is the standard language for relational databases: SELECT reads, INSERT adds, UPDATE changes, DELETE removes, and JOIN stitches tables together. It has been the lingua franca of data for fifty years and is still on every data job description. 🗃️", {"keys": ["sql language", "structured query language"]}),
    ("nosql", "NoSQL covers non-relational databases — document stores like MongoDB, key-value stores like Redis, wide-column systems like Cassandra, graph databases like Neo4j. They trade SQL's strict schema and joins for flexible shapes and horizontal scale, which is the right call for some workloads and the wrong one for many. 🍃", {"keys": ["no-sql"]}),
    ("postgresql", "PostgreSQL is a battle-tested open-source relational database: ACID transactions, JSON support, extensions for geospatial and vector data, and three decades of reliability. It is the default serious answer when someone asks which SQL database to use. 🐘", {"keys": ["postgres"]}),
    ("mongodb", "MongoDB is a document database: data lives in JSON-like objects with flexible shapes instead of rigid tables and rows. Its schema-less nature speeds up early development and shifts the discipline of consistency into application code. 🍃", {"keys": ["mongo"]}),
    ("a database index", "A database index is a lookup structure (usually a B-tree) that makes reads fast by avoiding full table scans — the cost is extra storage and slower writes, because every insert must update the index too. Index what you filter and sort by; ignore the rest. 📇", {"keys": ["indexes", "database indexes"]}),
    ("a database transaction", "A transaction groups several operations so they either all succeed or all roll back, keeping data consistent through failures and concurrent access. The classic example: transferring money debits one account and credits another — half of it must never happen. 💳", {"keys": ["transactions", "acid"]}),
    ("a query", "A query is a question you ask a database — in SQL it is a statement like SELECT name FROM users WHERE active; in other stores it is an API call. The difference between a fast and slow product is often the difference between a good and bad query. 🔍", {"keys": ["queries", "database query"]}),
    ("an orm", "An ORM (object-relational mapper) lets you query a database through your programming language's objects instead of raw SQL — convenient, typed, and portable. The catch: it can hide slow queries behind innocent-looking code, so good teams still know the SQL underneath. 🔄", {"keys": ["object relational mapping"]}),
    ("a data pipeline", "A data pipeline moves and transforms data through stages — ingest, clean, enrich, store, serve. Pipelines are where data quality is actually made: the fanciest dashboard is worthless if the pipeline quietly ingests nulls at 3 AM. 🚰", {"keys": ["data pipelines", "etl"]}),
    ("etl", "ETL (extract, transform, load) is the classic data pipeline pattern: pull data from sources, clean and reshape it, load it into a warehouse. Modern variants call themselves ELT — transform after loading, inside the warehouse's compute. 🔀", {"keys": ["extract transform load"]}),

    # ---- cloud & infra ----
    ("cloud computing", "Cloud computing rents computing instead of owning it: virtual machines, storage, databases, and services from providers like AWS, Azure, and GCP, billed by usage. The real change is elasticity — capacity appears when traffic spikes and disappears when it does not. ☁️", {"keys": ["the cloud", "cloud services"]}),
    ("serverless", "Serverless means you deploy functions and the provider runs them on demand, scaling from zero and billing per invocation. There is still a server — you just never see or manage it — which is why the name is a small lie with a large convenience. ⚡", {"keys": ["serverless functions", "functions as a service", "lambda"]}),
    ("docker", "Docker packages an application with everything it needs to run — code, runtime, libraries, config — into a container image, so it behaves identically on your laptop and in production. \"It works on my machine\" ends when the machine ships inside the box. 🐳", {"keys": ["containers", "containerization"]}),
    ("kubernetes", "Kubernetes orchestrates many containers across many machines: it schedules workloads, restarts failed pods, scales replicas, and handles rolling updates. It is powerful and famously complex — most teams should earn their way into it. ☸️", {"keys": ["k8s"]}),
    ("a virtual machine", "A virtual machine simulates a whole computer in software, running its own operating system on shared hardware. VMs give strong isolation and portability; containers are the lighter-weight successor for most app workloads. 🖥️", {"keys": ["virtualization", "vms"]}),
    ("a microservice", "Microservices split an application into small independently deployable services that talk over the network — separate teams, separate release cycles. The benefits are organizational; the costs are distributed-systems problems, which is why many teams wisely stop somewhere short of a hundred services. 🧩", {"keys": ["microservices"]}),
    ("a monolith", "A monolith is an application deployed as one unit. It is unfashionable but often correct: one codebase, one deploy, simple calls — until the team and load grow big enough that independent pieces would help. \"Monolith first\" is standard advice from people who have cleaned up both directions. 🏰"),

    # ---- languages & tools ----
    ("a framework", "A framework is a skeleton that calls your code — it defines the structure of an application and runs the show, while you fill in the specifics. React, Django, and Unity are frameworks; a library is the opposite: you call it. The inversion of control is the whole distinction. 🏗️", {"keys": ["frameworks", "framework vs library"]}),
    ("a library", "A library is a collection of code you call when you need it — you stay in control of the flow. Libraries compose; frameworks dictate. Every dependency is also a liability, which is why experienced developers are picky about them. 📚", {"keys": ["libraries", "software library"]}),
    ("an sdk", "An SDK (software development kit) is a bundle of tools for building on a platform — libraries, documentation, samples, and command-line utilities. \"Use our SDK\" means \"we have wrapped the hard parts of our API for you.\" 🧰", {"keys": ["software development kit"]}),
    ("npm", "npm is Node.js's package manager and the world's largest software registry — millions of packages of JavaScript. It is a superpower (anyone can build anything) and a supply-chain risk (any dependency can become malware), so lockfiles and audits matter. 📦", {"keys": ["node package manager", "nodejs package"]}),
    ("a package manager", "A package manager installs, updates, and removes software libraries along with their dependencies, keeping versions consistent. npm, pip, cargo, and apt are examples — the lockfile that records exact versions is the unsung hero. 📦", {"keys": ["package management", "dependency manager"]}),
    ("html", "HTML (HyperText Markup Language) describes the structure of a web page: headings, paragraphs, links, images, forms. It is markup, not programming — but its element semantics are what screen readers and search engines actually consume. 🧱", {"keys": ["html language", "hypertext markup language"]}),
    ("css", "CSS (Cascading Style Sheets) styles web pages: layout, color, type, animation, and how everything adapts to different screens. The \"cascading\" part — which rule wins when several apply — is the language's superpower and its most common source of confusion. 🎨", {"keys": ["css styling", "stylesheets"]}),
    ("javascript", "JavaScript is the programming language of the web: every browser runs it, and with Node.js it runs servers, tools, and desktop apps too. Written in 10 days in 1995, it has quirks nobody would design today — and an ecosystem nobody can ignore. 🟨", {"keys": ["js language"]}),
    ("typescript", "TypeScript is JavaScript plus static types: it catches whole bug classes before code runs and documents intent in signatures. It compiles down to plain JavaScript, so it runs everywhere JS does — which is how it became the default for large web codebases. 🔵", {"keys": ["ts language"]}),
    ("python programming", "Python is a readable, high-level language famous for its clean syntax and enormous ecosystem of data and AI libraries. It is usually the first language recommended to beginners and the last one used by researchers — a rare double life. 🐍", {"keys": ["python language", "programming in python"]}),
    ("a programming language", "A programming language is a formal vocabulary for instructing computers, with syntax (how it is written) and semantics (what it means). Hundreds exist because they optimize for different things: speed, safety, brevity, teachability — nobody writes an operating system in the same language they prototype data analysis in. 🗣️"),
    ("low level and high level languages", "Low-level languages like assembly and C map closely to the hardware — fast and explicit, but verbose and unforgiving. High-level languages like Python and JavaScript abstract the machine away, trading some speed for developer time — and developer time is usually the expensive part. ⚙️", {"keys": ["low level language", "high level language"]}),
    ("assembly", "Assembly is a low-level language of human-readable instructions matching a CPU's machine code: move this value, add these registers, jump there. Nobody writes whole applications in it anymore — it survives in firmware, compilers, and code where nanoseconds matter. 🔩", {"keys": ["assembly language", "asm"]}),
    ("garbage collection", "Garbage collection automatically frees memory that is no longer reachable — Java, Python, Go, and JavaScript do it for you. The trade: less control and occasional pauses, versus C's model where forgetting one free() is a crash or a security hole. ♻️", {"keys": ["garbage collector", "memory management"]}),
    ("a memory leak", "A memory leak is memory that a program keeps holding but can never use again — a forgotten cache, an unremoved listener, a growing map. Short-lived programs shrug it off; servers slowly strangle until someone stares at a rising heap graph. 📈", {"keys": ["memory leaks"]}),
    ("a race condition", "A race condition is a bug where the outcome depends on the timing of concurrent operations — two threads both reading \"balance = 100\" before either writes \"withdraw 100\" can both succeed. They are the hardest bugs to reproduce because the failure needs a specific interleaving. 🏁", {"keys": ["race conditions"]}),
    ("a deadlock", "A deadlock is when two or more processes each wait forever for a lock the other holds — like two people stepping aside in a narrow hallway, mirroring each other forever. Prevention: consistent lock ordering, timeouts, or designs that avoid shared locks. 🔒", {"keys": ["deadlocks"]}),
    ("a buffer overflow", "A buffer overflow happens when a program writes past the end of allocated memory, corrupting adjacent data — historically one of the most exploited vulnerability classes in C and C++. Memory-safe languages like Rust make this category impossible by construction. 🛡️", {"keys": ["buffer overflows"]}),
    ("regex", "A regular expression is a mini-language for matching text patterns: \\d+ finds digit runs, ^start anchors, groups capture. Regex is unbeatable for parsing and validation — and famously turns unreadable when pushed beyond its weight class. 🔍", {"keys": ["regular expression", "regular expressions"]}),
    ("unicode", "Unicode assigns a unique code point to every character in every writing system — over 149,000 of them, from A to emoji to historic scripts. UTF-8 encodes those code points as bytes for storage; it is why the same text file works on every platform, and why \"character count\" is a harder question than it sounds. 🌐", {"keys": ["utf-8", "character encoding", "encoding"]}),
    ("a url", "A URL is a web address with structure: scheme (https), host (github.com), path (/pfarell), and optional query (?tab=repos) and fragment (#readme). Understanding the parts is how you debug redirects, trackers, and broken links. 🔗", {"keys": ["urls", "web address"]}),
    ("a domain name", "A domain name is a human-readable address that maps to an IP via DNS — like pfarell.github.io. Names are hierarchical (subdomain . domain . top-level domain) and registered through registrars; the name is rented annually, never truly owned. 🌐", {"keys": ["domain names", "domains"]}),
    ("the dom", "The DOM (Document Object Model) is the browser's live tree of a web page: every HTML element becomes a node that JavaScript can read and change. React and other frameworks are essentially efficient DOM-updating machines — because touching the real DOM is slow. 🌲", {"keys": ["document object model"]}),
    ("a browser", "A web browser is an operating system in disguise: it fetches resources, parses HTML/CSS/JS, lays out pixels, runs a JavaScript engine, manages tabs as processes, and sandboxes everything for safety. Chrome, Safari, Firefox, and Edge are engineering marvels with user-hostile revenue models attached. 🧭", {"keys": ["browsers", "web browser"]}),
    ("responsive design", "Responsive design makes one page work across screens — phones, tablets, desktops — using flexible layouts, fluid images, and media queries that rearrange content by viewport. It replaced building separate mobile sites because maintaining one design is hard enough. 📱", {"keys": ["responsiveness", "mobile friendly"]}),
    ("deployment", "Deployment is getting code from a repository into production where users can reach it: build, copy, configure, start, verify. Boring deployments are a feature — small steps, automated checks, instant rollback — because every dramatic deployment story is bad news. 🚀"),

    # ---- security & misc ----
    ("phishing", "Phishing tricks people into revealing credentials or installing malware — a convincing fake login page, a \"package delivery\" text, a poisoned search result. Technical defenses help, but the attack targets attention; slowing down before clicking is the real control. 🎣", {"keys": ["phishing attacks", "scam email"]}),
    ("malware", "Malware is software designed to harm or exploit: viruses spread by infecting files, worms self-replicate across networks, trojans hide inside legitimate-looking programs, ransomware encrypts your data and demands payment. Updates and backups are the boring, effective answer. 🦠", {"keys": ["virus computer", "ransomware", "computer virus"]}),
    ("ransomware", "Ransomware encrypts your files and sells you the key. The modern advice is blunt: restorable offline backups are the only reliable defense, and paying funds the next attack. Most incidents start with a phishing email or an unpatched service. 🔒", {"keys": ["ransom ware"]}),
    ("an api key", "An API key is a secret string that identifies your application to a service — like a password for programs. Keys belong in environment variables and secret stores, never in client-side code or Git: anything shipped to a browser is public, and leaked keys get scraped and abused within minutes. 🔑", {"keys": ["api keys", "secrets"]}),
    ("rate limiting", "Rate limiting caps how many requests a client can make in a time window — protecting servers from overload, abuse, and brute-force attacks. Good APIs return 429 Too Many Requests and tell you when to retry. 🚦", {"keys": ["rate limits", "throttling"]}),
    ("web scraping", "Web scraping extracts data from websites programmatically — fetching pages and parsing their HTML. It is how price trackers and research datasets are built, and it lives in a gray zone: public data is often fair game, but terms of service, robots.txt, and personal data laws draw the real lines. 🕷️", {"keys": ["scraping", "scraper"]}),
    ("a bug", "A bug is a defect where code does not do what it should. The term predates computers — Edison used it for engineering faults — and the modern version was popularized when Grace Hopper's team taped an actual moth into a logbook in 1947. 🐛", {"keys": ["software bug", "bugs in code"]}),
    ("error handling", "Error handling is how a program responds when things go wrong — invalid input, network failure, missing file. Good handling fails loudly in development, gracefully in production, and never silently swallows exceptions; silent catch blocks are where bugs go to hide. ⚠️", {"keys": ["exceptions", "exception handling"]}),
    ("a linter", "A linter statically analyzes code for suspicious patterns, style violations, and likely bugs before anything runs — undeclared variables, unused imports, accidental equality mistakes. It is cheap insurance, and its real gift is ending style arguments by fiat. 🧹", {"keys": ["linters", "linting"]}),
    ("a hackathon", "A hackathon is a time-boxed build event — usually 24–48 hours — where teams prototype ideas and demo them to judges. The winning strategy is not the most ambitious idea but the most convincingly finished demo; Farell's Chorémon won MLH's Best Use of Gemini API at one. ⏱️", {"keys": ["hackathons"]}),
]
